PVAccess discovery uses UDP broadcast which doesn't pass through Docker's NAT well.

Try --network host instead of port forwarding:


docker run --rm --name va-test --network host va-digital-twin

The fundamental issue is that on macOS, Docker runs inside a Linux VM — UDP broadcast from container → host doesn't work the same as on a real Linux machine. In Kubernetes (real Linux pods on the same network), this won't be a problem.

macOS Docker networking blocks PVA. Let's confirm it works inside the container:


docker exec va-test python -c "
from p4p.client.thread import Context
ctx = Context('pva')
val = ctx.get('BPMS:IN20:581:TMIT', timeout=5)
print('BPMS:IN20:581:TMIT =', val)
"

If that returns a value, your PVA server is working correctly — it's just that Docker Desktop on Mac runs a Linux VM and PVA UDP discovery/connections can't bridge to the host. This won't be an issue in Kubernetes where pods share real network namespaces.

The pvget timeout from your Mac is purely the Docker Desktop VM networking layer — PVA's UDP discovery doesn't bridge to macOS. In Kubernetes on real Linux, pods on the same network will reach each other fine.